# decision pyproject.toml uses minimal line-oriented edits

The PyPI adapter edits only the matching `[project].dependencies` item and
`[tool.uv.sources]` key. A general TOML marshal is not used because the available small Go TOML
libraries do not preserve comments and formatting. The editor refuses an unsupported table
shape instead of rewriting the document. An inline `[project].dependencies` array is converted
to the equivalent multiline array only when an entry must be added; unrelated arrays and TOML
text remain byte-for-byte unchanged. Fixtures cover inline and multiline PEP 621 arrays, existing
and new uv source tables, idempotent wiring, and unwiring. Drift inspection is scoped to the
requested export's dependency/source entry and compares both the canonical locked Git URL and the
full locked commit; it must not accidentally match another git dependency elsewhere in the file.
When the matching PEP 621 requirement already exists, wiring replaces that item in place if its
git source or locked revision changed; mere presence of the project name does not make it current.
UV source keys may be bare or single-/double-quoted TOML keys. Matching consumes horizontal
indentation only, never the newline after `[tool.uv.sources]`, so replacing a key cannot join it
to the table header or create a quoted duplicate beside a bare key.

Poetry projects with `[tool.poetry.dependencies]` use Poetry's native inline Git table. PEP 621
projects whose `dependencies` field is absent gain a delimited field that unwire removes as a
unit; this supports valid projects with dynamic or intentionally empty runtime dependencies
without reconstructing `pyproject.toml`.
Every variant writes `LockedDependency.Git` and the full `LockedDependency.Commit`: uv uses
`rev`, Poetry uses its native inline Git table, and PDM/plain PEP 621 use a direct Git
requirement.

# decision Pull synchronizes a usable Python environment

Pull checks the saved manager through the shared prerequisite contract and synchronizes a usable
project environment. uv runs `uv sync --no-install-project`. Poetry first runs `poetry lock`
without updating unchanged locked packages and then `poetry sync --no-root`; PDM first runs
the targeted `pdm update --no-self --update-reuse NAME`, because a plain reuse lock keeps an old
same-version VCS revision. PDM removal uses its targeted `pdm remove --no-self NAME` operation,
which reconciles the declaration, lock, and selected environment together; plain sync does not
remove a distribution merely because it disappeared from the lock. If the interpreter selected
by the project-local `.pdm-python` is a missing conventional venv target, Pull recreates exactly
that venv; it never deletes, cleans, or retargets an existing external/shared environment. Plain PEP 621 owns a project-local
`.venv` and pip installs the exact direct Git requirement with normal dependency resolution;
the direct requirement is force-reinstalled so a new commit with the same package version is
applied, then a normal pip install of all declared project requirements repairs a newly recreated
environment without broadly upgrading already-satisfying unrelated packages. `--no-deps` is
forbidden. Remove edits only the owned requirement/source and reconciles the same
native lock/environment, or uninstalls the direct plain-pip distribution without broadly cleaning
shared caches or orphaned transitive packages.
Unsupported subdirectories are rejected by read-only Capability before editor side effects;
missing tools are errors and never trigger adapter fallback.

# invariant Inspect uses native Python evidence

Inspect is offline and read-only. It discovers uv's configured project environment, Poetry's
project interpreter, and PDM's selected interpreter (including external project environments)
instead of assuming `.venv`. For uv, Poetry, and PDM it requires the matching native lock package
entry at the locked Git source and full commit. Every variant verifies the installed distribution's
PEP 610 `direct_url.json`; a matching directory name is never sufficient evidence. Missing or
stale manager-owned lock/materialization is repairable by Pull, while declaration drift is not.
